 - Chosen design → Profiles are chains (r0, superseded r1): research Q3 resolved — op order pinned with per-action single-touch (A14), `based-on` is `derives[0]`, no alias, and the unit of `MAX_CHAIN_DEPTH` is one `derives` link, so the ceiling is a base plus 32 patches. `fork`/`merge` keep their disjoint-set rule as the `patch/v1/spec.md` contract and land with the ADR-012 caller; `diff` ships (r1: the algebra had no v1 consumer). `seeded-from` requires `for` at parse, and a `for` naming no manifest in the index is `UnresolvedAncestor`, so `resolve`'s preset check and the drift gate never run against nothing (r3: a grammatical pin only guaranteed a pin, `Resolved.manifest: None` read a dangling pin as an unbound profile, and an unpinned `seeded-from` was vacuously green — one rule closes both).
 - Chosen design → Profiles are chains → Id → file (r0, amended r1): ids resolve through a walked index, never a path, keeping the shipped escape-id fixture valid (A18); the manifest index joins it keyed on each document's declared `game` + `revision`, so a pin is a string lookup and no path is built from a pin (r1, same class as A18); the walk's limits are the public `MAX_INDEX_ENTRIES` / `MAX_INDEX_DEPTH` with `IndexTooLarge` / `IndexTooDeep` (r1: an unnameable limit is no limit, and they are new budget, not reused); entries are taken by `DirEntry::file_type()` with the cap on bytes read; two manifest documents declaring one `game` + `revision` are `DuplicateId`, so a pin is never ambiguous and the drift gate stays deterministic (r2: the manifest carries no `id`, so only the declared key can collide); `UnresolvedAncestor` is stated once as a document reference with no document — a `derives` entry, **or a `for` pin naming no manifest** (r3: a dangling pin was unruled and read as the `manifest: None` of an unbound profile) — and each walked tree accepts only the formats it holds, a document declaring another being `InvalidValue` at index time (r3: a manifest-format document under `data/profiles/` reached the index missing the key that tree indexes by); file names follow the stem rule, with the extension stated separately for the `schemas/` fixtures and the `data/` manifest, placement, and policy documents.
-- Chosen design → Transforms (r1, amended r2): the policy's required `hand` and the `mouse-swap` knob are deleted — the invocation carries the hand, and listing a transform is the opt-in (a required entry no consumer reads costs a major bump to remove). The closed invocation table — each transform's name, arity, and per-argument grammar — is `policy/v1/spec.md`'s, and `apply_policy`, not core's reader, validates the whole `stack` and refuses a mismatch as `InvalidValue`, as the `Err` of `Result<PolicyOutcome, CoreError>` with `skipped` left as the inapplicable-transform report, and Test #10 asserts the registry equals the table (r2: a parse-time check sat on the far side of the one-way dependency edge, so core had to duplicate the registry; r3: the declared `-> PolicyOutcome` had no error channel, so a rejected stack was indistinguishable from an applied one with skips). `relocate-cluster`'s precondition is isometry to the placement's source-key set (replacing the undefined "contiguous cluster"), and `generated-by` is a `<namespace>:<part>` id over the closed producer list `policy|preset|generator`, which enforces no free-text attribution (r2: the shape never refused `author:alice`, so the privacy claim was overstated).
