 - Chosen design → Manifests (r0, superseded r1): axis kind stays canonical in the taxonomy with `AxisKindConflict` as the cross-check, and emphasis stays one statement — the manifest's `default` row is the game fact, the reach class is its reading. The role-qualified prefix keys are replaced by the `action`/`default`/`exclusive` nested-array rows, so the closed-entry-set rule is never relaxed and its prefix enumeration, fixture, and Risk row are gone; `validate` keeps only manifest facts and the profile's `seeded-from` check moves to `resolve` (r1: a profile fact is not a manifest signature's to evaluate). `docked` keeps only its `action`-row membership check, and honoring it is stated as apply-time work in Deferred (r2: the "a binding move cannot escape it" claim named no reader in v1, so the entry carried an unenforced guarantee).
-- Chosen design → Adversarial posture (r0, amended r1): the research's untrusted-document and path-traversal risks consolidated here and answered by reuse — the shipped budget, closed entry sets, bounded messages, per-format dotted-key fixtures; the per-document budget is unchanged, the tree walk adds the two named constants (A25), and the id-index rule replaces id-charset narrowing. A variant is pinned by a `fixtures/invalid/` document only where `parse` can see the condition, and the multi-document and loader-level ones over fixture data by the suite that calls their producer (#1, #3, #4, #6, #8, #9) — `MergeConflict` ships with `fork`/`merge`, `IndexTooLarge` / `IndexTooDeep` with Test #1's synthetic walk, `DuplicateId` and `UnresolvedAncestor` with #9 — and the authoring bar's "one invalid fixture per rejected condition" and Test #10's fixture parenthetical were deleted with that claim (r3: the shipped harness asserts one fixture file through `parse`, so "each pinned by a fixture" held only of parse-visible conditions, and `DuplicateId` was missing from its exception list). `schemas/README.md`'s guard section gains the two constants and their rejections (r2: the README's "three constants" claim needed the new limits in it).
-- Files created / Rollout (r1, amended r2): `schemas/README.md` gains the admitted keyboard-name list as the `keyboard:` vocabulary the reach loader enforces — Test #3 iterates the content-derived graded set, not the list, so no full-keyboard grading obligation returns — plus the guard section's two index limits and their rejections, and the data-homes rule (contract tree = fixed-path lookup tables, `data/` = walked content), which replaces the `repo-practices` line; `serialize` arms ship so every format round-trips, and the drift and model-walk suites share one `Index` per test binary.
